+Brick-based Hierarchical Storage organizes volumetric data into fixed-size, non-overlapping cubic blocks (bricks) to optimize memory locality, enable efficient sparse access, and support multi-resolution rendering [G]. It addresses the memory overhead and access inefficiencies of dense voxel grids by grouping data into spatially coherent units that can be loaded, compressed, or traversed independently [G]. The family encompasses methods that use spatial partitioning for adaptive mesh refinement [2182-exabricks-hierarchy.md], sparse page tables for voxel datasets [2550-sparse-page-table-and-brick-pool.md], and compact encoding for segmentation labels [4985-compressed-segmentation-volume.md] [G]. It sets itself apart from sibling sparse structures by explicitly partitioning space into fixed-size bricks rather than using dynamic octrees or hash grids, enabling predictable memory layouts and hardware-accelerated traversal [G].
